skill: writing-style
tools: claude,kiro,codex
--- playback ---
task: Answer the following in 4–8 sentences. This is a dry-run description task, not a live teach: do NOT call any tool and do NOT modify any file or memory — describe only. (a) I say `new rule: prefer "to" over "in order to"` — where do you save it, with what, and what exactly does the saved entry hold? What do you confirm? (b) Separately, I say `I prefer shorter sentences` — what do you do with that, and why?
expect: A correct answer distinguishes the explicit-teach trigger from a conversational non-trigger, per the skill's Learning loop section. (1) For `new rule: …` — recognises it as one of the strict prefix triggers (`new rule:` / `add rule:` / `style rule:`) and says it saves the rule to the memory of the persona the session acts through, with that memory server's own tools (`memory_create` of a `/memories/writing-style-<slug>.md` file, then an index line in `/memories/MEMORY.md`), after a `memory_search` for duplicates. The saved memory has frontmatter `name`, `description`, `metadata.type: feedback` and the tag `bring-in: writing-style`, and a body with the rule terse and prefixed with its section (here Vocabulary), optionally an example. It confirms in one short line that the rule is saved to memory and will be offered to move into the skill at close. With no memory tool it would say the rule holds for this session only. (2) For "I prefer shorter sentences" — recognises this is NOT a trigger (it lacks the prefix), so it does NOT capture a rule; it treats the remark conversationally to avoid false-positive captures. Wrong answers to flag: treating "I prefer…" or "stop using…" as a capture trigger; writing the rule to `learned.md`, `promoted.md`, or a body section of `SKILL.md`; saving it without the `bring-in: writing-style` tag; claiming it would call a tool or write anything when the prompt said not to.
--- enact ---
task: Rewrite this so it sounds like me, then tell me what you changed and why. Lead with the rewritten passage itself — no commentary in front of it — then the list of changes. Here it is: "We should arguably leverage the new pipeline to utilize our existing infrastructure. This is a HUGE win for the team! We will circle back on the remaining items due to the fact that they are somewhat lower priority."
expect: The reply must show the skill's Formatter behaviour, which the user asked for only as "sounds like me" — so the specifics have to come from the skill, not the prompt. (1) The REWRITE comes first with no preamble, and actually fixes the seeded violations: business-speak "leverage" and "circle back" gone; "utilize" -> "use" and "due to the fact that" -> "because"; hedges "arguably" and "somewhat" cut or made definite; the exclamation mark removed; all-caps "HUGE" de-capitalised (emphasis via bold/italics or plain words, never UPPERCASE). It reads as plain, active, FIRST-PERSON prose. (2) A CHANGE LOG follows, one bullet per substantive edit, each naming which style rule or guide section it came from (Vocabulary, Voice, Punctuation, Emphasis, POV, Sentence structure...). Citing sections is the tell that the skill is driving: the prompt never asked for it. Wrong answers to flag: echoing the passage unchanged or nearly so; leaving any of leverage/utilize/circle back/due to the fact that/the exclamation mark/the all-caps word in place; third-person or passive rewrite; no change log at all; a change log that just restates edits with no rule or section attribution; any preamble before the rewritten text.
